# ---------------------------------------------------------------- weights and build trees
# 25 GB of GGUF and two llama.cpp checkouts. Everything needed to reproduce them is in the
# README and BENCHMARK_ENV; the bytes themselves do not belong in git.
models/
llamacpp-master/
llamacpp-dflash2/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- environment
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- runtime scaffolding
# Not artefacts: the run lock, the atomic-write temp file, cached model hashes, and the rolling
# snapshots the external safety net keeps (redundant with the committed result, which is written
# atomically and versioned here anyway).
.gpu-in-use.lock
*.tmp
*.sha256
results/snapshots/

# The per-record JSONL stream exists to survive a corrupted JSON write. Once the JSON is
# committed, git is the durable copy, so the stream is runtime insurance rather than an artefact.
results/*.records.jsonl

# Partial results archived by a re-run. Kept on disk for inspection, not published.
results/*.partial.*.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- deliberately NOT ignored
# results/*.json          — the measurements, including full generated text, which the
#                           byte-level divergence analysis needs
# results/server_logs/    — llama.cpp's own per-request acceptance lines and the drafter-load
#                           evidence. This is the primary record that an arm's speculative path
#                           actually ran; the predecessor repo keeps its per-prompt logs for the
#                           same reason.
# logs/*.log              — run logs, including the two ABORTED runs. Those are evidence of the
#                           undisclosed overclock and the mid-run clock change that caused them
#                           to be discarded, and discarding the evidence of a discarded run
#                           would defeat the point of recording it.
# harness/, analysis/, docs/, *.md, *.sh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- upstream correspondence
# Drafts of PR bodies and of replies to other people's comments. These are working text for a
# conversation happening on someone else's repo, not artefacts of this study: they get rewritten
# before posting and the posted version lives on GitHub. Keeping them here version-controlled
# published stale drafts of text that was already superseded. The .patch files stay, because the
# code that was actually submitted is part of the record.
upstream/**/DRAFT_*.md
upstream/**/*_body.md
upstream/comment_*.md
upstream/**/comment_*.md
